/code-review high — R5 round 3 — branch 2026-09-20-pack-first-run-safety (range b43a91ef..75103af5)

## Findings

1. MEDIUM — …/mise/tasks/code/git-config:97 (the --fix branch, and the check call that ends it)
The hook runs --fix, which rewrites user.name/user.email/user.signingkey/commit.gpgsign in .git/config and then returns 0 — but git has already loaded its config before the pre-commit hook fires, so the commit that triggered the hook is authored and signed with the previous settings. Verified empirically: with a global "Old Name <old@example.com>" and a pre-commit hook that writes the local identity, the first commit lands as Old Name and only the second uses the new one. Scenario: a clone on a machine with GITHUB_EMAIL=work@… and a global personal address — the first commit is pushed from the personal address, unsigned if commit.gpgsign was off globally, while the hook printed OK. The docs written in this diff (task-library.md:192, conventions.md:114) claim the repo "is corrected on its first commit", which is false for that commit. Fix: track whether --fix changed any key and exit 1 with "identity corrected — re-run the commit" when it did. (Round 1 logged this as INFO/pre-existing; it is now load-bearing because the hook writes an identity rather than removing one, and the docs assert the opposite.)

2. LOW — …/tasks/setup/precommit:38-49 (LOCAL_HOOKS_PATH / GLOBAL_ONLY)
The global/system case is only recognised when no local value exists. If core.hooksPath is set both locally (husky) and globally, the task classifies it as local, --force unsets the local value, then pre-commit install --overwrite fails with pre-commit's own "Cowardly refusing" — the task has already done its destructive step and never prints its by-hand --global --unset-all line. Probe --global/--system scopes (or re-read the effective value after the local unset) before installing.

3. LOW — …/tasks/setup/precommit:54 (for path in .husky lefthook.yml .lefthook.yml)
lefthook also reads lefthook.yaml, lefthook.toml, lefthook.json and the .lefthook.{yaml,toml,json} forms. A repo using any of those is not detected as foreign, so the plain path installs (chaining as .legacy, not destructive) and --force overwrites it without the refusal the docs promise.

4. LOW — …/tasks/code/git-config:35-38 (the case "$HOST" forge match)
Exact/lower-case only. An SSH host alias (git@github.com-work:org/repo.git, the standard multi-account setup) or a mixed-case host (GitHub.com) resolves to GIT, so the hook demands GIT_* on a GitHub repo and flips to GITHUB_* once the alias is normalised. Consistent with ruling 2 as literally written; either lower-case the host or document that alias hosts fall to GIT_*.

